From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] Keep the row expression alive across -removeRowForVariable:

-[GSCSTableau removeRowForVariable:] reads the expression for a row out
of the _rows dictionary, removes the entry -- which releases the
expression, since the dictionary is its only owner -- and then walks
the freed expression's term variables. Its caller,
-pivotWithEntryVariable:exitVariable:, fetched the same expression a
line earlier and goes on to rewrite it and put it back into the tableau
under the entry variable, all on a freed object.

Every content-view resize of a window that has a GSAutoLayoutEngine
goes through -[GSAutoLayoutEngine updateContentViewSize], which removes
and re-adds the size constraints, and removing a constraint pivots. So
any resize of such a window is a use-after-free whose outcome depends
on what the allocator has since put in the chunk: a segfault in fast
enumeration over the freed term array, a silently corrupted tableau, or
nothing at all. The crash surfaces as a memcpy fault in
-[GSMutableArray countByEnumeratingWithState:objects:count:] below
-removeRowForVariable:, under -[NSView setFrame:] on the window
decoration view.

A window gets an engine without ever asking for Auto Layout. On GNUstep
-layoutSubtreeIfNeeded is not a no-op for a constraint-free window: it
runs -updateConstraints over the subtree,
translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints is YES by default, so every
view's autoresizing mask becomes a set of
NSAutoresizingMaskLayoutConstraints, the first of which creates the
window's GSAutoLayoutEngine. An application that calls
-layoutSubtreeIfNeeded once after building its view hierarchy -- a
common Cocoa idiom for "lay out now" -- is therefore on the Cassowary
solver from then on, and dies when the window is maximized, or when a
window manager resizes the window as it maps it.

Retain the expression for the current autorelease scope before the row
is removed -- the pattern that -[GSCassowarySolver
removeConstraintFromTableau:] already uses for its marker variable.

gnustep-gui's NSLayoutConstraint, NSLayoutAnchor, NSLayoutGuide and
NSCollectionViewFlowLayout suites pass with the change, as does the
rest of Tests/gui, 4,675 tests.

A reproduction program, gscstableau-removerow-use-after-free.m,
accompanies this patch: a window of plain autoresizing views, one
-layoutSubtreeIfNeeded and a resize. Valgrind names the freed object on
the first resize and MALLOC_PERTURB_ turns it into a segfault every
time; with the change it is clean.
---
Keep the row expression alive across -[GSCSTableau removeRowForVariable:]

-[GSCSTableau removeRowForVariable:] reads the expression for a row out of
the _rows dictionary, removes the entry -- which releases the expression,
since the dictionary is its only owner -- and then walks the freed
expression's term variables. Its caller, -pivotWithEntryVariable:exitVariable:,
fetched the same expression a line earlier and goes on to rewrite it and
put it back into the tableau under the entry variable, all on a freed
object.

Every content-view resize of a window that has a GSAutoLayoutEngine goes
through -[GSAutoLayoutEngine updateContentViewSize], which removes and
re-adds the size constraints, and removing a constraint pivots. So any
resize of such a window is a use-after-free whose outcome depends on what
the allocator has since put in the chunk: a segfault in fast enumeration
over the freed term array, a silently corrupted tableau, or nothing.

A window gets an engine without ever asking for Auto Layout: on GNUstep
-layoutSubtreeIfNeeded runs -updateConstraints over the subtree, which
translates every autoresizing mask into NSAutoresizingMaskLayoutConstraints
and creates the engine. XFormsViewer's document window does exactly that,
and dies on maximize.

Retain the expression for the current autorelease scope before the row is
removed -- the pattern -[GSCassowarySolver removeConstraintFromTableau:]
already uses for its marker variable.

A standalone reproduction is in gscstableau-removerow-use-after-free.m:
under valgrind, "Invalid read ... removeRowForVariable: ... free'd by
removeObjectForKey:" on the first resize; with MALLOC_PERTURB_ set, a
segfault. With this patch it is clean, and gnustep-gui's NSLayoutConstraint,
NSLayoutAnchor, NSLayoutGuide and NSCollectionViewFlowLayout suites pass.

diff --git a/Source/GSCSTableau.m b/Source/GSCSTableau.m
index a4167466e..4f97dcb9e 100644
--- a/Source/GSCSTableau.m
+++ b/Source/GSCSTableau.m
@@ -214,6 +214,13 @@
                  userInfo: nil];
       [missingExpressionException raise];
     }
+  /* The row dictionary is the expression's only owner.  Removing the entry
+   * releases it, but its term variables are still needed below, and
+   * -pivotWithEntryVariable:exitVariable: goes on to rewrite the very same
+   * expression after this method returns.  Keep it alive for the rest of
+   * the current autorelease scope.
+   */
+  expression = AUTORELEASE(RETAIN(expression));
   [_rows removeObjectForKey: variable];
   if ([variable isExternal])
     {
